# Foliage — local chief-of-staff (apps/cos)
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. NOTHING IN THIS FILE IS A REAL VALUE, and nothing in this
# file is a default: every line here is a placeholder.
#
# The name has no leading dot ON PURPOSE. Every `.env*` in this repository is gitignored
# with NO NEGATIONS, because a negated allow-list ("ignore .env*, except .env.example") is
# exactly how the previous repository ended up tracking a real `.env` — the negation was
# added for the example file and then a real one matched it. See AGENTS.md.


# --- Tier 1: the device-only store ------------------------------------------------------
# Where the encrypted store file lives. Device-local. Never sync this path into a cloud
# folder: the file is ciphertext, but a synced ciphertext is still a copy you did not choose
# to make, and it moves the file out of the threat model localStore.ts actually defends.
FOLIAGE_COS_STORE=

# The secret the Tier 1 key is derived from (scrypt, N=2^17 — see apps/cos/src/localStore.ts).
#
# READ THIS BEFORE SETTING IT. An environment variable is the WEAKEST of the acceptable ways
# to supply this secret: it is visible to every child process, it lands in shell history and
# in `ps` output on some systems, and process managers log it. It is documented here because
# a non-interactive run needs some way in, not because it is recommended. Prefer a prompt on
# a terminal. Foliage never writes this value anywhere, never transmits it, and never stores
# a key derived from it — losing it means the store cannot be opened by anyone, including you.
FOLIAGE_COS_SECRET=


# --- The declared egress policy ---------------------------------------------------------
# A COMMA-SEPARATED SET OF KIND NAMES, NOT A LEVEL. There is no value of this that means
# "more" — it names which declared kinds may leave the machine, and nothing else.
#
#   tier2-confidential | tier3-shareable
#
# `tier1-local` is REFUSED here. Naming device-only material as exportable is wrong the
# moment it is written, so the router refuses the policy on sight rather than on the day it
# would first have mattered.
#
# UNSET AND EMPTY BOTH MEAN THE DEVICE-ONLY POSTURE, and that is the posture a person gets
# by not having decided yet — which is the correct one to get. Nothing crosses.
FOLIAGE_COS_MAY_CROSS=


# --- Tier 3: escalated inference (Fireworks) --------------------------------------------
# ABSENT ON PURPOSE IS A SUPPORTED STATE. With no credential the router still runs, still
# classifies, still computes the egress set, and refuses with `no-credential` — which is a
# different, visible outcome from a policy refusal. Leaving these blank is a real
# configuration, not a broken one.
#
# THE `FOLIAGE_` PREFIX IS LOAD-BEARING, and this file said otherwise until the Integrate
# phase ran the thing. `apps/cos/src/fireworks.ts` reads ONLY the prefixed names below. The
# unprefixed `FIREWORKS_API_KEY` / `FIREWORKS_BASE_URL` / `FIREWORKS_MODEL` are treated as
# AMBIENT — some other tool's Fireworks credential that happens to be exported in this
# shell — and are detected and NAMED IN THE REFUSAL rather than silently used. Foliage does
# not spend a credential it was not handed on purpose.
#
# This file previously documented the unprefixed trio, which is the exact failure that
# header warns about, arriving from the documentation instead of the shell: fill it in,
# run, and get `no-credential` listing variables the file never mentioned.
FOLIAGE_FIREWORKS_API_KEY=
FOLIAGE_FIREWORKS_MODEL=

# Optional. Defaults to https://api.fireworks.ai/inference/v1 — a constant rather than a
# required variable because it is documentation, not a secret. Override it to point at a
# dedicated deployment (spec §5) without editing code.
FOLIAGE_FIREWORKS_BASE_URL=

# There is deliberately NO timeout variable. The deadline is the caller's argument
# (`FireworksRequest.timeoutMs`, default 60s), and a documented variable that nothing reads
# is worse than an undocumented one — a person acts on it and nothing happens.


# --- Local inference (Ollama on loopback) ------------------------------------------------
# THE HEADLINE OF THE LOCAL PATH. With these set, a `local-only` request is ANSWERED on this
# machine: the router keeps everything here, and the model that reads it is here too.
#
# NO DEFAULT MODEL NAME EXISTS, and that is deliberate. A default would be this code deciding
# which model reasoned about you. Unset means unset: the run says so, names the variable, and
# falls through to the deterministic floor — which still answers.
FOLIAGE_LOCAL_MODEL=

# Optional. Defaults to http://127.0.0.1:11434 — the IP literal rather than `localhost`, so
# the path this product takes by itself resolves no names at all.
#
# LOOPBACK IS ENFORCED ON THE PARSED HOSTNAME, twice: once when the configuration is read and
# again immediately before the only fetch, so a hand-built config or an edited constant fails
# CLOSED. `http://127.0.0.1.evil.com/` is REFUSED — its host is a subdomain of evil.com and
# only its first characters look like the loopback address. So is `http://127.0.0.1@evil.com/`,
# whose host is evil.com with the literal hidden in the userinfo. `0.0.0.0` is refused too: it
# is a bind address, not a destination.
#
# AND A REDIRECT IS REFUSED RATHER THAN FOLLOWED. Checking the URL we ASK for is not checking
# the one we end up at: Node follows redirects by default, and a 307 preserves the method and
# the body, so whatever is listening on this port could otherwise answer `307 Location:
# http://somewhere-else/` and this client would carry your whole prompt there. Every 3xx is
# reported as its own cause and no hop is taken — including a redirect that points back at
# loopback, because the guarantee is about the connection actually made.
#
# WHAT LOOPBACK PROVES, EXACTLY: that this process did not dial off-box. It does NOT prove the
# bytes stop there. Whatever is listening on that port is another program and can forward
# anything it receives — that is a property of what you ran, not of this check.
FOLIAGE_LOCAL_MODEL_BASE_URL=

# There is deliberately NO timeout variable here either. The deadline is the caller's argument
# (`LocalModelRequest.timeoutMs`, default 120s). 120s is arithmetic, not habit: a 32.8s cold
# model load was measured on the machine this was built against, and a reflexive 10s would
# report a healthy runtime as broken.
